;; The module doc of std/prelude.bjo, read through (:doc #:file
;; "prelude.bjodoc"). The prelude's names are documented by area in the
;; prelude-*.bjodoc files beside it. The texts are samizdat markup: code in
;; prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "What every module has without importing anything: collections, strings, files, ports, concurrency and the macros the language is written with.")
  (see println ->str fold map list-map vec-map map-ref str file-read-text read-line sync with-scope cond)
  (example """
    (defun (main)
      (def words (string-split (file-read-text "notes.txt") " "))
      (println #"${(length words)} words")
      0)
    """)
  (reference """
    Every module outside the standard library imports @code{(std prelude)}
    without saying so, unless it imports it itself, with modifiers, to hide or
    rename part of it. Its names come from three
    places: the definitions in @code{std/prelude.bjo} and the files it
    includes, the names it re-exports from @code{(std eq)} and
    @code{(std maths)}, whose docs are in those modules, and the builtins
    compiled into the runtime, whose docs are here.

    @strong{Collections.} @code{List} is the immutable linked list,
    @code{Vec} the persistent vector, @code{Map} the persistent hash map, and
    .NET arrays are there for interop and speed. Each has functions of its
    own, named after it (@code{list-map}, @code{vec-ref}, @code{map-set}),
    and a builder or a transient for building one in a loop.

    @strong{Iteration.} The traits @code{Iterable}, @code{Collection},
    @code{Cursor} and @code{Collector} are what @code{loop} and the generic
    @code{fold}, @code{map}, @code{filter} and @code{length} work through, so
    those work on every collection, on strings, on ranges and on lazy
    @code{Seq}s.

    @strong{Strings and characters.} A string is UTF-8, and positions in one
    are cursors rather than indices. A @code{char} is a Unicode scalar value.

    @strong{Files and ports.} Files are read and written whole, or through
    ports. The file system is reached through the @code{FS} effect, so a test
    can replace it, and logging through the @code{warn} and @code{log}
    effects.

    @strong{Concurrency.} Bjoroutines run on fibers, which meet on channels
    through Concurrent ML events (@code{sync}, @code{choose}, @code{wrap}),
    inside scopes that wait for them, cancel them, and raise their failures
    (@code{with-scope}, @code{with-deadline}).

    @strong{Macros.} @code{cond}, @code{if-let}, @code{some->}, @code{get},
    @code{type/derive} and the reader extensions @code{#map} and @code{#ch} are
    macros, and the syntax functions macro transformers use are here as well.
    """))
